        '6-8', $q$The Age of Canals$q$, $q$(1) Before railroads spread across the country, moving heavy goods over land was slow and expensive. (2) Wagons on dirt roads could carry only small loads, and the roads turned to mud after rain. (3) In the early 1800s, many communities began digging canals, which are human-made waterways built for boats. (4) A canal boat was usually pulled by mules or horses that walked along a path beside the water. (5) This path was called a towpath. (6) One team of animals could pull a boat holding far more than a wagon could carry.

(7) Canals had to cross hills and valleys, so engineers built locks. (8) A lock is a chamber with gates at each end. (9) Workers closed the gates and let water in or out, which raised or lowered the boat to the next level. (10) Locks are kind of cool. (11) Towns along the canals grew quickly because farmers could now ship grain to distant cities.

(12) Railroads became faster than canal boats and could keep running in winter when canals froze. (13) Many canals closed, but some old towpaths are now used as trails for walking and biking.$q$, 'draft')
