insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('213a83d0-b572-52ba-9ee3-408f996b5c97', 'wg8-informative-writing-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g8-informative-writing'), null,
        '6-8', $q$How a Phone Knows Where It Is$q$, $q$(1) When a phone shows a blue dot on a map, it is using the Global Positioning System, or GPS. (2) GPS is a network of satellites that circle Earth and send out radio signals. (3) Each signal carries the exact time it left the satellite and the satellite's position.

(4) A GPS receiver, like the one inside a phone, picks up these signals. (5) It measures how long each signal took to arrive. (6) Since radio signals travel at a known speed, the receiver can figure out how far away each satellite is. (7) By combining distances from at least four satellites, the receiver calculates its location. (8) This process is called trilateration.

(9) GPS has many uses beyond giving directions. (10) Farmers use it to guide tractors in straight rows. (11) Rescue teams use it to find hikers who have lost their way. (12) Scientists place receivers on glaciers to measure how slowly the ice moves. (13) GPS is good.$q$, 'draft')
