        '6-8', $q$How an Ocean Island Is Born$q$, $q$(1) Some islands in the middle of the ocean began as nothing more than cracks in the seafloor. (2) Deep below the crust, a pocket of melted rock, called magma, pushes slowly upward. (3) When the magma breaks through a crack, the cold seawater cools it, and it hardens into dark rock. (4) At last, the top of the rocky pile rises above the waves, and a new island appears. (5) Over thousands of years, eruption after eruption adds new layers on top of the old ones.

(6) Bare rock is not a friendly home for living things. (7) Seeds drift in on ocean currents, ride on the wind, or travel tucked in the feathers of birds. (8) Hardy plants take root in cracks where rainwater collects and bits of soil build up. (9) Insects and birds soon follow, because the plants offer them food and shelter. (10) Over time, the gray rock turns green as more and more living things arrive and settle in.$q$, 'draft')
