insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('25e5d099-6c79-527b-ae98-20202416c70a', 'wg9-argument-writing-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g9-argument-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$City Bus Passes for Gilmore Students$q$, $q$(1) Students at Gilmore High School who live more than two miles away ride yellow school buses, but those buses leave at 3:10 every afternoon. (2) Students who stay for clubs, tutoring or sports practice must find their own way home. (3) The district should give every Gilmore student a free pass for the city bus system. (4) The city bus stops in front of the school every fifteen minutes until nine at night. (5) In a survey by the Gilmore student council, 210 students said they had skipped an after-school activity at least once because they had no ride home. (6) In Oak Mesa, a city in another state, schools began offering free passes to high school students, and attendance at after-school tutoring rose by a third in one year.

(7) Some people say passes are bad. (8) The program would cost the district about $45,000 a year. (9) That amount is less than the district spends running two afternoon activity buses, which carry only a dozen riders on most days. (10) Replacing those two buses with passes would save money while giving every student more choices.

(11) Free passes would also teach students to travel on their own before they graduate. (12) Many students will soon ride buses to jobs, college classes and internships, and learning the routes now will make that easier. (13) The bus seats are kind of uncomfortable, but it's no big deal. (14) In the end, free bus passes would let more students stay after school, cost less than the current system and prepare students for life after high school.$q$, 'draft')
